What is a Load Balancer?
A load balancer is like a traffic controller for your app. It makes sure requests from users are evenly distributed across multiple servers (or pods), so no single server gets overloaded.


Scenario :
Imagine you run an online movie ticket booking app.
On a normal day, 100 users visit — one server can handle it easily. 
Suddenly, a big blockbuster movie is released. 10,000 users try to book tickets at the same time. 

If all requests hit one server, it crashes — users see errors, app is down. 
Enter the Load Balancer. It splits traffic across multiple servers/pods. Some requests go to Server A, some to Server B, some to Server C.
If Server B fails, traffic automatically goes to healthy servers, keeping the app alive and fast.

Result: All users can book tickets without delays, even under huge traffic spikes. 



Key Takeaway:
Load balancers ensure your app is scalable, reliable, and fault-tolerant, especially during high traffic or failures.
